    "text": "The WorldLines paper introduces memory-to-plan translation solely as a mechanism to mitigate the effects of partial observability and state overwriting, not as an independently necessary challenge for embodied agents in general.",
    "topic": "Memory-to-plan translation scope"
  },
  {
    "text": "No section of the WorldLines paper argues that memory-to-plan translation is a universal requirement for all embodied agents; its necessity is always conditioned on the partial observability constraints assumed in the paper.",
    "topic": "Universality of the challenge"
  },
  {
    "text": "The description of memory-to-plan translation in the WorldLines paper is embedded within the system architecture designed to handle memory overwrites, confirming it is an engineered solution rather than a standalone problem statement.",
    "topic": "Architectural role"
  }
